# herdr session context

This agent runs in a pane of a herdr session. herdr is the multiplexer and
agent orchestrator; Neovim is the editor. Verify with `test "${HERDR_ENV:-}" = 1`
before using any of this; your coordinates are `$HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID`,
`$HERDR_TAB_ID` and `$HERDR_PANE_ID`.

## Showing the user a file

Use `nv <path>` (equivalently `herdr-nvim <path>`). It opens the file in the
Neovim that owns this workspace, focuses that pane, and blocks until the user
closes the tab. Never start a bare `nvim` — that creates a second editor in
your own pane, with none of the user's buffers or LSP clients.

To read a file for yourself, read it directly rather than opening it.

## Running shell commands

Run them in your own pane. When something needs to keep running, or the user
should watch it, put it in a sibling pane instead of blocking yourself:

```bash
herdr pane split --current --direction right --cwd "$PWD" --no-focus
herdr pane run <returned-pane-id> "<command>"
herdr pane read <returned-pane-id> --source recent-unwrapped --lines 120
```

Split right from a wide pane, down from a tall one, and keep `--no-focus` so
the user is not yanked out of what they were doing.

## Interpreting user references

- "the editor", "my editor", "nvim" — the Neovim pane in this workspace.
- "the other pane", "next to you" — a sibling pane in the current tab; find it
  with `herdr pane list --workspace "$HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID"`.
- "this workspace", "this project" — `$HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID`, one checkout.
- "the agents" — `herdr agent list`, which reports each one's state.

Do not close panes, tabs or workspaces you did not create, and never run
`herdr server stop`.
